  "body": {
    "id": "thm:hall-full-rank-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The fortified pairing has generic full row rank if and only if \\(\\kappa_0(S)=0\\) and \\(\\dim_{\\mathbb F_S}(\\sum_{(y,j)\\in I}W_y)\\ge|I|\\) for every \\(I\\subseteq\\mathcal D_S\\). Equivalently, generic full row rank holds if and only if \\(\\kappa_0(S)=0\\) and \\(\\eta(S)=0\\). Failure of either condition is a law-uniform failure of this pairing route, not a claim that \\(\\tau\\) is unidentified.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(\\kappa_0(S)\\)",
      "\\(\\mathbb F_S\\)",
      "\\(W_y\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal D_S\\)",
      "\\(\\eta(S)\\)",
      "\\(\\tau\\)",
      "\\(M_\\theta\\)",
      "\\(\\Theta_S\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:generic-rank-formula"
    ],
    "justification": "The all-subfamilies inequalities are the exact structural-support frontier rather than a global supply-versus-demand count.",
    "gap": "Published proximal rank conditions are law-level conditions, and generic partitioned-matrix theorems allow different parameter dependence than the stochastic product blocks here.",
    "consumer": "The frontier gives a deficient subset of treatment-by-complement blocks that explains why an apparently adequate aggregate alphabet cannot support fortified completeness.",
    "proof_tex": "The generic-rank formula gives\n\\[\nr_{\\mathrm{gen}}(S)=Q_S-\\kappa_0(S)-\\eta(S).\n\\]\nBoth terms subtracted are nonnegative integers: \\(\\kappa_0(S)\\) is a dimension, and \\(\\eta(S)\\ge0\\) because the empty set in its defining maximum has value zero.  Hence \\(r_{\\mathrm{gen}}(S)=Q_S\\) if and only if both terms vanish.\n\nFor \\(I\\subseteq\\mathcal D_S\\), write\n\\[\n\\rho(I)=\\dim_{\\mathbb F_S}\\left(\\sum_{(y,j)\\in I}W_y\\right).\n\\]\nBy definition,\n\\[\n\\eta(S)=\\max_{I\\subseteq\\mathcal D_S}\\{|I|-\\rho(I)\\}.\n\\]\nSince the maximum includes the value zero at \\(I=\\varnothing\\), it is zero exactly when \\(|I|-\\rho(I)\\le0\\) for every \\(I\\), which is exactly the displayed family of Hall inequalities.  This proves both equivalences.\n\nIf either condition fails, the last part of the generic-rank theorem shows \\(\\operatorname{rank}M_\\theta<Q_S\\) at every \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_S\\).  This conclusion concerns only injectivity of the fortified pairing map.  The frozen assumptions contain neither a pair of observationally equivalent structural causal models with different \\(\\tau\\) nor a completeness theorem asserting that this pairing condition is necessary for every possible identification route.  Therefore the proved implication is precisely law-uniform failure of this pairing route and does not logically imply nonidentification of \\(\\tau\\).",
